{"qid":"Q189545","names":["Zengid dynasty"],"title":"Zengid dynasty","endonym":null,"capsule":"The Zengid dynasty was a Turkic atabeg line founded by Imad ad-Din Zengi, who seized Mosul from the Seljuk governors in 1127 and captured the Crusader county of Edessa in 1144 — the first significant rollback of Crusader territorial gains. His son Nur ad-Din (r.1146–1174) unified Syria under Zengid rule, established Aleppo and Damascus as twin capitals, and sponsored the military career of Saladin, who ultimately eclipsed the dynasty by founding the Ayyubid sultanate in 1174. Zengid successor branches persisted in Sinjar and the Jazira until Mongol and Ayyubid pressure extinguished the last lines by the mid-thirteenth century.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Zengid territorial extent is reasonably attested through Arabic chronicles and Crusader sources for the Syrian core; the Jazira appanages are less precisely mapped and boundaries with competing atabegs are approximate.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Zengid dynasty","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zengid_dynasty"},{"label":"Britannica: Saladin (covers Zengid context)","url":"https://www.britannica.com/biography/Saladin"}],"britannica":"topic/Zangid-dynasty","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Zengid dynasty","rulers":[{"qid":null,"name":"Imad al-Din Zengi I","nativeName":"عماد الدين زنكي","roman":"Imad al-Din Zangi I","start":1127,"end":1146,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense":null},{"qid":null,"name":"Saif al-Din Ghazi I (Mosul)","nativeName":"سيف الدين غازي","roman":"Sayf al-Din Ghazi I","start":1146,"end":1149,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense":null},{"qid":null,"name":"Nur al-Din Mahmud (Aleppo)","nativeName":"نور الدين محمود","roman":"Nur al-Din Mahmud","start":1146,"end":1174,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense":null},{"qid":null,"name":"Qutb al-Din Mawdud (Mosul)","nativeName":"قطب الدين مودود","roman":"Qutb al-Din Mawdud","start":1149,"end":1170,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense":null},{"qid":null,"name":"Saif al-Din Ghazi II (Mosul)","nativeName":"سيف الدين غازي الثاني","roman":"Sayf al-Din Ghazi II","start":1170,"end":1180,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense":null},{"qid":null,"name":"Imad al-Din Zengi II (Sinjar)","nativeName":"عماد الدين زنكي الثاني","roman":"Imad al-Din Zangi II","start":1170,"end":1197,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense":null},{"qid":null,"name":"al-Salih Ismail (Aleppo)","nativeName":"الصالح إسماعيل","roman":"al-Salih Ismail","start":1174,"end":1181,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense":null},{"qid":null,"name":"Izz al-Din Mas'ud I (Mosul)","nativeName":"عز الدين مسعود","roman":"Izz al-Din Masud I","start":1180,"end":1193,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense":null},{"qid":null,"name":"Nur al-Din Muhammad I (Aleppo, brief)","nativeName":"نور الدين محمد","roman":"Nur al-Din Muhammad I","start":1181,"end":1183,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense":null},{"qid":null,"name":"Nur al-Din Arslan Shah I (Mosul)","nativeName":"نور الدين أرسلان شاه","roman":"Nur al-Din Arslan Shah I","start":1193,"end":1211,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense":null},{"qid":null,"name":"Izz al-Din Mas'ud II (Mosul)","nativeName":"عز الدين مسعود الثاني","roman":"Izz al-Din Masud II","start":1211,"end":1218,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense":null},{"qid":null,"name":"Nasir al-Din Mahmud (Mosul)","nativeName":"ناصر الدين محمود","roman":"Nasir al-Din Mahmud","start":1218,"end":1219,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense":null},{"qid":null,"name":"Badr al-Din Lu'lu' (Mosul, as regent then lord)","nativeName":"بدر الدين لؤ